---
title: Privacy policy
description: Which personal data nullish processes, why, where, and for how long.
---

This policy explains which personal data we process when you use nullish or visit this website, why, where and for how long.

## Who we are

Fluster GmbH, c/o The Hub Zürich Association, Sihlquai 131, 8005 Zürich, Switzerland, is responsible for the processing described here.

For any question about your data, write to [hi@nullish.ch](mailto:hi@nullish.ch).

## Applicable law

We process personal data in accordance with the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection (FADP) and, where it applies, the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).

## What we process

**Account**: your name, email address, password (stored hashed), language and two-factor authentication settings.

**Organizations**: the data you and your team enter in nullish, such as clients, invoices, bank statements, journal entries and VAT returns. It may include personal data about your clients or suppliers. We process it on your behalf, only to provide nullish.

**AI assistant**: your questions and the data the assistant reads to answer them.

**Subscription**: your plan, its status and your invoices. Card details are entered at Stripe, never in nullish.

**Technical data**: IP address, browser and server logs, needed to run nullish and keep it secure.

**Website**: visits to this website are counted without cookies, and the data is anonymized.

## Why

- To provide nullish and its features.
- To manage your subscription and billing.
- To send emails linked to your account, such as sign-in verification or a trial reminder.
- To keep nullish secure and fix problems.
- To meet our legal obligations, such as keeping our accounting records.

We don't sell your data, don't use it for advertising and don't use it to train AI models.

## Where and with whom

Your accounting data is stored and processed in Switzerland. We work with these providers:

| Provider                                           | Purpose             | Location                    |
| -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- | --------------------------- |
| [Infomaniak](https://www.infomaniak.com)           | Server and AI model | Switzerland                 |
| [cloudscale](https://www.cloudscale.ch)            | Encrypted backups   | Switzerland                 |
| [Amazon Web Services](https://aws.amazon.com/ses/) | Sending emails      | Switzerland (Zurich region) |
| [Stripe](https://stripe.com)                       | Payments            | Ireland, United States      |

Stripe only receives what it needs to process payments. Transfers to countries without an adequate level of data protection rely on the European Commission's standard contractual clauses.

More details on the [Hosting](/en/data/) page.

## How long

- Your account and organizations are kept as long as you use nullish.
- When an organization is deleted, its data is deleted for good 30 days later.
- Backups are encrypted and rotated. Data deleted from nullish disappears from them as the old backups expire. The longest kept are yearly backups, for up to 10 years.
- Our own invoices and accounting records are kept for 10 years, as required by Swiss law.
- Server logs are kept for a short time, then deleted.

## Security

All traffic goes over HTTPS and the server's disk is encrypted. Backups are encrypted before they leave the server. Only the nullish team can access the servers, with a hardware security key.

## Cookies

nullish only uses the cookies needed to keep you signed in. There are no tracking or advertising cookies.

## Your rights

You can ask to access, correct or delete your personal data, to receive it in a common format, or object to its processing. Write to [hi@nullish.ch](mailto:hi@nullish.ch).

For the data in your organizations, the organization is responsible. Requests from its clients or suppliers should go to it first, and we help it answer.

You can file a complaint with the Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner ([FDPIC](https://www.edoeb.admin.ch)) or, in the EU, with your local supervisory authority.

## Changes

We may update this policy. For important changes, we let you know by email.
